From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giga Edge SDK <noreply@giga-edge-sdk.invalid>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 13:08:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Use pre-packed weights saved with the model without
 re-packing

With session.save_external_prepacked_constant_initializers a session can
save pre-packed weights next to the model, but loading still runs PrePack()
on the source weights and only then looks the result up in the saved data.
The new session option
session.use_prepacked_constant_initializers_without_repack ("0" by default)
lets supported CPU kernels take the saved buffers directly: the source
weights are not read and nothing is packed again, which saves load time and
peak memory.

- OpKernel gets two hooks: GetPrePackedWeightsFormat() names the packing
  format of an input, and AcceptPrePackedMetadata() checks the saved buffers
  against the tensor metadata and sets up the kernel without reading the
  tensor data. Gemm (SGEMM), LSTM and the MatMulInteger family (QGEMM)
  implement them; MLAS reports its packing formats through
  MlasGemmPackBFormat(), versioned and tied to the selected kernel.
- The writer aligns every blob to kAllocAlignment and adds prepacked_v2_*
  entries that key the buffers by operator, input, type, shape and format.
  Older readers skip these entries.
- The loader maps (or reads) the saved blobs from the external data file.
  Files passed with AddExternalInitializersFromFilesInMemory are used in
  place: both the source and the packed weights point into the caller's
  buffer.
- Missing, stale or incompatible entries fall back to the regular PrePack()
  path. Buffers owned by the caller are never shared across sessions.

Two existing tests now expect aligned blobs and an ONNX Runtime error
instead of a filesystem exception for a missing external data file.
